1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:08,100
if we now just have a rapid look at the different
services of an operating system we can see

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:12,469
that there are parts of the operating system
that are above the system calls so you don't

3
00:00:12,469 --> 00:00:14,030
really need to interact with the hardware

4
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:19,220
the graphical user interface for example
here is an example and parts that are below

5
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:24,150
so the file system when you want to store/open
a file and this kind of things you need to actually

6
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:31,369
interact with the disk so this stays
let's say below the level of the system calls

7
00:00:31,369 --> 00:00:38,640
there are parts of the operating system that
are there for the user to basically interact

8
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:43,011
with the hardware so you can run programs
that you need to read from the disk you can

9
00:00:43,011 --> 00:00:46,170
communicate with devices you can access files
and so on

10
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:52,500
there are parts of the operating system that
are actually about dealing with errors

11
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:59,710
a connection failure so if you try to attempt to read a
part of memory

12
00:00:59,710 --> 00:01:01,059
you don't have the right to read

13
00:01:01,059 --> 00:01:07,150
and of course there are parts of the operating
system that are for the operating system itself

14
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:12,610
to be efficient to decide which processes
to run to protect the memory of a user

15
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:14,850
from other users and so on and so forth

